  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Easterly gap winds strengthen tonight into Sunday morning.\n   Anticipate choppy lakes and strong canyon winds. \n\n-  Widespread rain and mountain snow above 7,000 feet late tonight\n   into early Monday, mainly through central Idaho and northwest\n   Montana.\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\nSatellite imagery early this afternoon across the Northern Rockies\nhighlights a cirrus cloud shield moving overhead under a westerly\nflow aloft. A broad low pressure system is seen off the Oregon\ncoast. Easterly winds observed earlier this morning across \nwestern Montana will weaken and shift to the west-northwest by \nmid-afternoon into this evening. Cooler temperatures will be felt\nacross northwest Montana and along the divide today as the \neasterly gradient this morning ushered in a colder airmass than \nfelt previously the last few days. \n\nLooking ahead, the low pressure system will move inland tonight\ninto Sunday morning, tracking across eastern Washington, before\nmoving along the USA-Canadian border Sunday night into early \nMonday morning. Easterly winds will once again develop after \nsunset tonight across western Montana and will trend stronger than\nlast night. The strongest gusts (30-45 mph) will be felt on \nwestern slopes of the Whitefish, Swan, and Mission mountains and \nin canyon/gap locations. The winds will generally peak during the \nlate evening to early morning hours on Sunday as the low tracks \ninto eastern Washington and the pressure gradient is maximized. \nThose planning to head outdoors Sunday morning should be prepared\nfor choppy conditions on area lakes and cool, blustery conditions\nin the backcountry. \n\nThe low pressure's northern track continues to focus precipitation\namounts across north-central Idaho and northwest Montana, where \nhalf an inch to around an inch of rain is forecast across the \nhigher terrain, with lesser amounts (0.10-0.50 inches) in valley \nareas. Snow levels will fall near 7,000 feet by Sunday night in \nthese areas. Backcountry recreationists should prepare for cold, \nwet, and raw conditions that will significantly increase the risk \nof hypothermia for anyone caught unprepared. Further south and \neast, including the Missoula area, precipitation amounts will be \nnotably less (< 0.10 inches). Southwest Montana, east of the \ndivide (east and south of I-15 and I-90), will remain dry with \ngusty southwest winds, resulting in critical fire weather, where a\nRed Flag Warning is in place for Sunday afternoon and evening. \n\nThe system will depart to the east by Tuesday, transitioning the \nregion to a generally warmer and drier weather pattern through \nmid-week. Despite the overall drying trend, a few weak atmospheric\ndisturbances may still trigger isolated, light showers. Tuesday \nmorning stands out as having the potential to be one of the\ncoldest mornings yet this Fall. The higher valleys of southwest \nMontana and areas along the Continental Divide will see low \ntemperatures near or below freezing. Frost is also a potential \nconcern for the Missoula and Bitterroot Valley and higher mountain\nvalleys within the Clearwater Mountains of Idaho.",
